Total working capital includes all current assets and liabilities; operating working capital excludes financing items by a defined perimeter.|Receivables ₹30 + inventory ₹50 − payables ₹25 gives operating investment ₹55.|Adding financing debt to this operating definition is…?|Inconsistent|Required|Irrelevant to any analysis
Cash conversion cycle measures time between supplier cash payment and customer collection.|CCC = inventory days + receivable days − payable days.|50 + 30 − 40 days gives…?|40 days|120 days|20 days
Days ratios translate average operating balances into time using matched sales or cost denominators.|Receivables ₹20 on annual credit sales ₹365 imply about 20 days.|Inventory days normally uses…?|Cost of sales|Net profit|Equity value
Cash budgets forecast dated receipts, payments, and minimum balances.|₹10 opening cash + ₹5 receipts − ₹20 payments leaves a ₹5 shortfall.|A positive annual profit eliminates this shortfall?|No|Yes|Only if audited
Credit policy trades sales against collection delays and default; collections turn recognised receivables into cash.|Relaxing terms raises sales but lengthens customer payment time.|Evaluate…?|Incremental contribution, financing, and loss costs|Only revenue growth|Only invoice count
Inventory financing funds stock using loans or asset-based facilities.|₹50 stock supports borrowing subject to eligible collateral and lender discounts.|All inventory is equally eligible collateral?|No|Yes|Only if insured
Supplier terms affect funding and discounts; compare early-payment benefits with financing cost.|Paying ₹98 now instead of ₹100 later saves ₹2 but uses cash earlier.|The decision depends on…?|Timing, alternative funding cost, and liquidity|Only the nominal discount|Only supplier size
Factoring transfers receivables and collection or credit functions; invoice discounting provides receivable-backed funding, with terms varying.|A factor advances ₹90 against ₹100 invoices and charges fees.|Recourse means the seller may retain…?|Credit exposure|No obligation ever|Ownership of the customer's factory
Reverse factoring lets suppliers finance approved buyer invoices using a structured programme.|A supplier receives early payment while the buyer settles later.|Buyer disclosure should consider…?|Funding nature, terms, and liquidity exposure|Only supplier revenue|Only payment branding
Overdrafts and revolvers provide reusable short-term liquidity subject to limits and conditions.|A ₹100 revolving limit with ₹30 drawn leaves ₹70 undrawn before conditions.|Undrawn capacity always equals immediately usable cash?|No|Yes|Only at quarter-end
Commercial paper is short-term market borrowing that may require rollover and backup liquidity.|A firm must refinance maturing paper next month.|The central risk is…?|Refinancing access|Only depreciation|Only inventory valuation
Cash pooling combines group cash management; intercompany funding requires legal, tax, and solvency analysis.|One subsidiary's surplus supports another's deficit through approved arrangements.|Consolidated surplus guarantees every subsidiary can transfer cash freely?|No|Yes|Only within one group
Treasury investment policies prioritise permitted risk, liquidity, maturity, and counterparty limits.|Payroll cash is placed in a volatile long-duration asset.|Which objective is threatened?|Capital preservation and liquidity|Only revenue growth|Only branding
FX and rate exposures arise from currency flows and variable-rate funding.|Dollar imports and floating-rate debt expose a firm to exchange and rate changes.|A hedge should be matched to…?|Exposure amount, timing, and basis|Only last year's profit|Only bank preference
Liquidity buffers and contingency plans cover plausible funding interruptions.|Expected outflows ₹50 and reliable funds ₹35 imply a ₹15 gap before actions.|A plan should identify…?|Usable sources and realistic timing|Only book equity|Only historical profit
Treasury controls separate approvals, execution, reconciliation, and counterparty oversight.|One employee initiates, approves, and reconciles payments.|This primarily raises…?|Operational and fraud risk|Only market beta|Only tax expense
